INT. DEEP-SEA CORE LAB - NIGHT

A pressurized steel cylinder, lit a sickly amber. Pipes sweat. Through a single thick porthole, the black water presses close, and something pale drifts past and is gone.

DR. SABINE ARGYLE (40s, cropped hair, a sleeve of burn scars) kneels at a sample drawer. At the console, her partner TEODOR MARSH (50s, round glasses) taps a failing readout. Between them hovers the lab's AI, VESPER, a small amber light in a ceiling lattice.

The floor shivers. A bolt pings off the wall.

SABINE
That wasn't the pump.

TEODOR
That wasn't the pump.

VESPER (V.O.)
Hull stress at nine percent above tolerance.

The two scientists exchange a look. Sabine slams the drawer shut; Teodor lunges for the console. They collide in the narrow aisle, tangling arms, and both catch the same railing as the lab pitches.

SABINE
Vesper, which section?

TEODOR ^
Vesper, which section?

VESPER (V.O.)
Aft. The sample bay.

The porthole darkens. A pale shape presses against it, slow, deliberate. The shiver in the floor becomes a groan.

SABINE
Seal it.

TEODOR
Seal it now!

They throw their shoulders against the bulkhead wheel together, and it turns, screeching, and the amber light flickers and holds.
